 "text": {
  "markets": {
   "title": "9 of 21 markets hold 79% of revenue and brought 89% of the increase",
   "sub": "Each bubble is one market (customer country)\nAcross: net revenue in Jul 1997–Apr 1998, in US$ thousands (log scale)\nUp: change in net revenue vs Jul 1996–Apr 1997, in US$ thousands\nBubble size: number of active customers\nBlue bubble: no single customer brings half of the market's revenue\nOrange bubble: one customer brings half or more of the market's revenue\nDashed lines: medians, which divide the four quadrants",
   "src": "Bubble scatter. Data: Orders, Order Details, Customers. n = 21 markets",
   "about": "Each market placed by its size and its dollar growth. The dashed medians divide the four quadrants.",
   "takeaway": "The 9 Invest markets hold 79% of revenue and brought 89% of the increase. USA, Germany and Brazil rest on 12, 11 and 9 customers respectively, while Austria, Sweden, Ireland and Switzerland each depend on one dominant account. The 8 Deprioritize markets together bring 9% of revenue."
  },
  "categories": {
   "title": "Dairy Products added the most dollars; Seafood grew fastest (+193%)",
   "sub": "Net revenue by product category, in US$ thousands\nLight blue dot: Jul 1996–Apr 1997\nBlue dot: Jul 1997–Apr 1998\nPercentage: growth between the two periods",
   "src": "Dumbbell chart. Data: Order Details, Product, Categories. n = 8 categories",
   "about": "A dumbbell chart: each category's revenue in the earlier period and the later period, joined by a line.",
   "takeaway": "Beverages and Dairy Products are the largest categories and added $136.9K combined. Seafood grew fastest, from $30.7K to $90.0K. Confections grew slowest, at +56%."
  },
  "team": {
   "title": "Each employee handled 53 orders on average, up from 31, across 17 countries",
   "sub": "Work handled by each employee, Jul 1996–Apr 1997 → Jul 1997–Apr 1998\nOrders: orders the employee handled\nCustomers: distinct customers on those orders\nCountries: countries those customers are in\nRevenue: net revenue of the orders handled in Jul 1997–Apr 1998",
   "src": "Table. Data: Orders, Employees, Customers. n = 9 employees",
   "about": "Orders, customers and customer countries each employee handled in both periods.",
   "takeaway": "Orders per employee rose from 31 to 53 on average (+74%), and customer countries per employee from 13 to 17. Each employee served customers in 11 to 20 countries. For a small team spread across all 21 markets, concentrating effort on the priority markets is the most direct lever."
  },
  "grid": {
   "title": "14 category gaps in priority markets are worth about $52.8K at the company-average mix",
   "sub": "Net revenue by market and category in Jul 1997–Apr 1998, in US$ thousands\nDarker blue: more revenue\nBold market name: priority market (Invest quadrant)\nOrange outline: in a priority market, the category earns under half its company-wide share of revenue\nDash: no sales",
   "src": "Heatmap. Data: Orders, Order Details, Customers, Product, Categories. n = 21 markets × 8 categories",
   "about": "Revenue for every market and category pair. Outlines mark where a priority market buys far less of a category than the company average.",
   "takeaway": "The 14 gaps add up to about $52.8K. The largest are Austria Beverages ($7.7K), USA Produce ($6.8K) and Brazil Meat/Poultry ($5.3K). Ireland alone has 5 gaps, consistent with a single customer buying a narrow range."
  },
  "finding": "9 of 21 markets combine above-median scale with above-median dollar growth; together they hold 79% of revenue and brought 89% of the increase. USA, Germany and Brazil pair that scale with a broad base of 12, 11 and 9 customers. Austria, Sweden, Ireland and Switzerland also rank highly, yet in each a single customer brings half or more of the market's revenue. Among categories, Dairy Products added the most dollars and Seafood grew fastest. The team is based in the UK and the USA, while 74% of revenue comes from other countries and each employee serves customers in 17 countries on average.",
  "implication": "Direct proactive sales effort to USA, Germany and Brazil, where scale, dollar growth and customer breadth coincide. Manage Austria, Sweden, Ireland and Switzerland as key accounts, since their results depend on one relationship each. Use the 14 category gaps as the cross-selling agenda for priority markets. Serve the 8 lowest-priority markets through standard order handling, and assign each priority market a named owner so that attention follows the portfolio.",
